WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:08.760
What's the best way to find the 'HCF' and 'LCM' of three numbers?

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:16.900
Let's say, we have to find the HCF an LCM of 70, 80 and 120.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:21.120
We have to use a technique which gives us both together

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.660
the LCM as well as the HCF.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:29.160
The best way to do that would be to reduce 70, 80

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.960
and 120 to its prime factors.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.080
70 equals '2 times 5 times 7'.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:44.860
80 is '16 times 5', which can be written as '2 raised to 4 times 5'.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:49.060
And 120 is '15 times 8',

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:55.160
which in prime factor form can be written as '2 cubed times 3 times 5'.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:56.900
What do we do now?

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:01.980
To keep it simple we can quickly make columns of the list of prime factors.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:06.960
We have 2, 3, 5 &amp; 7.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:15.100
In 70, we have '2 raised to 1', '5 raised to 1' and '7 raised to 1'.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:19.200
In 80, we have '2 raised to 4' and '5 raised to 1'.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:27.160
And in 120 we have '2 cubed, '3 raised to 1' and '5 raised to 1'.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:32.780
With this ready, we can easily find the HCF and the LCM.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:38.060
HCF is the product of the least powers of shared factors.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:42.000
As there are only two prime factors which are shared,

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:44.360
we look at only those two columns.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.520
The least power in this column is 1.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.720
So we write a '2 raised to 1'.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:54.000
And in this column as well the least power is 1.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.200
So we multiply this with '5 raised to 1'.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.220
And the product equals 10.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.220
The HCF of these three numbers is 10.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:08.780
For the LCM we take the highest power of each factor.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:11.560
The factors need not be shared.

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:14.800
The highest power in this column is 4.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:17.320
We write it a '2 raised to 4' here.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.680
In this column we have '3 raised to 1'.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:26.380
Why is this the highest? Because others are '3 raised to 0'.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:29.180
Third column, it's '5 raised to 1'.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:34.100
And the fourth column, has '7 raised to 1' as the highest power.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:38.580
This product equals 1680.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:43.920
That's the LCM of 70, 80 and 120.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.820
So we can see that with a simple column structure,

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:54.460
we can easily find the HCF and LCM of 3 numbers.

